WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.077
Hardware interfaces are where firmware
assumptions meet the physical device and its

2
00:00:05.157 --> 00:00:05.920
environment.

3
00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:09.623
GPIO, UART, SPI, I2C, CAN, USB, sensors,
actuators, storage parts,

4
00:00:09.703 --> 00:00:13.772
radio modules, and companion chips can all
provide data or control signals.

5
00:00:13.852 --> 00:00:18.365
Defensive firmware treats those interfaces
as boundaries that need validation and

6
00:00:18.445 --> 00:00:19.398
clear ownership.

7
00:00:19.478 --> 00:00:23.928
Peripheral data should not be blindly
trusted just because it comes from hardware.

8
00:00:24.008 --> 00:00:26.678
A sensor reading can be out of range,
stale, noisy,

9
00:00:26.758 --> 00:00:29.619
inconsistent with the current mode, or
unavailable.

10
00:00:29.699 --> 00:00:33.322
A bus message can have an unexpected
length, a bad state transition,

11
00:00:33.402 --> 00:00:37.661
an unsupported command, or a timing
pattern the application was not expecting.

12
00:00:37.741 --> 00:00:42.064
Drivers should separate low-level hardware
handling from application policy.

13
00:00:42.144 --> 00:00:45.958
A driver can normalize status, check basic
invariants, report errors,

14
00:00:46.038 --> 00:00:47.246
and expose a small API.

15
00:00:47.326 --> 00:00:51.966
Application logic can then make
higher-level decisions using validated data instead

16
00:00:52.046 --> 00:00:56.178
of repeating fragile register or protocol
details throughout the codebase.

17
00:00:56.258 --> 00:00:58.992
DMA needs careful buffer ownership at a
high level.

18
00:00:59.072 --> 00:01:01.932
If hardware can read from or write to
memory directly,

19
00:01:02.012 --> 00:01:05.063
software must know when a buffer belongs
to the hardware,

20
00:01:05.143 --> 00:01:08.576
when it belongs to the CPU, when cache or
alignment rules matter,

21
00:01:08.656 --> 00:01:09.991
and what length is valid.

22
00:01:10.071 --> 00:01:13.313
Simple happy-path tests may not expose
ownership mistakes.

23
00:01:13.393 --> 00:01:16.381
Actuator and control commands need strict
validation.

24
00:01:16.461 --> 00:01:19.830
A command path should define allowed
modes, parameter ranges,

25
00:01:19.910 --> 00:01:22.707
timing rules, authorization state where
relevant,

26
00:01:22.787 --> 00:01:25.012
and safe outcomes when validation fails.

27
00:01:25.092 --> 00:01:29.414
The design should avoid letting malformed
or out-of-state input drive physical

28
00:01:29.494 --> 00:01:30.066
behavior.

29
00:01:30.146 --> 00:01:34.215
Reviewers should ask practical questions:
where does this data originate,

30
00:01:34.295 --> 00:01:38.109
what range is valid, what mode must the
device be in, who owns the buffer,

31
00:01:38.189 --> 00:01:42.575
what happens on timeout or bus error, and
how is unexpected device state reported?

32
00:01:42.655 --> 00:01:46.660
Those questions make hardware-facing code
easier to secure and maintain.
